{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2082 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a553691d232f587e04a60eecb5aeed0a58acfed4f624bb3ebd2b3b4e2537ca07" :edb/db-before-t 2081 :edb/db-after-t 2082 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535394 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:17:01.089Z" 13194139535394 true] [17592186347044 1100 "$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\n$$\n\nThe triple integral above is defined over the type III region given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 2x,\\,0\\le y\\le xz}.\n$$\n\nFor a fixed admissible $x$, let $[a(x),b(x)]$ be the full nonempty $z$ fiber of the exactly specified region $S$. What is $a(x)+b(x)$?" 13194139535394 true] [17592186347044 1100 "$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\n$$\n\nThe triple integral above is defined over the type III region given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 2x,\\,0\\le y\\le xz}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535394 false] [17592186347044 1145 "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {u}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063571/assets/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,z)=0,\\,{u}_{2}(x,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}[{\\int }_{0}^{xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}{\\int }_{0}^{xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=(0)+(2x)=2x.\n$$" 13194139535394 false] [17592186347044 1145 "The defining set $S$ is retained exactly. The requirement $0\\le z\\le2x$ has no solution for $x<0$, so the actual outer range is $0\\le x\\le2$. For each such $x$, the full $z$ interval is $[0,2x]$, and $xz\\ge0$ makes the $y$ interval $[0,xz]$ nonempty. Hence\n\n$$a(x)+b(x)=0+2x=2x,\\qquad \\iiint_S f\\,dV=\\int_0^2\\int_0^{2x}\\int_0^{xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.$$\n\nFor $f=1$, the true volume is $\\int_0^2 2x^3\\,dx=8$. The source solution retained the nominal outer range $[-1,2]$ and included reversed $z$ bounds on negative $x$, giving the signed value $15/2$. That outer-bound error does not change the requested middle-endpoint sum $2x$." 13194139535394 true]] :edb/tempids {}}
